# x86_64 DBFS filesystem-server round-trip entry.
#
# The DBFS analogue of vfs_server_roundtrip_entry.spl. Proves that the SimpleOS
# DBFS FILESYSTEM SERVER is actually RUNNING inside the guest by pushing DATA
# through it and reading that same data back — not by printing a banner and not
# by observing that a path exists.
#
# Boot is OVMF pflash -> GRUB-EFI (a real EFI application) -> multiboot1 kernel.
# NEVER QEMU `-kernel`, NEVER isa-debug-exit: the point of the lane is that the
# same artifact could boot on hardware (.claude/rules/board-runnable.md).
#
# WHAT IS THE SERVER, AND WHY THIS EXACT CALL SEQUENCE:
# every step below is the PRODUCTION in-guest DBFS bring-up, taken from
# boot_fs.spl's `_try_dbfs_root_pure_simple` (src/os/kernel/boot/boot_fs.spl),
# using only public APIs:
#
#   vfs_boot_probe_pure_simple_nvme_adapter()  pure-Simple PCI -> NVMe grant ->
#                                              NVMe BlockDevice. The same
#                                              adapter boot_fs_sequence() hands
#                                              to its NVFS and DBFS probes.
#   dbfs_superblock_set_device / _probe_disk / _read_from_disk
#                                              the DBFS superblock at LBA 2.
#   DbFsDriver.open_on_device(dev, 4, blocks)  arena base LBA 4 == boot_fs.spl's
#                                              DBFS_ARENA_BASE_LBA.
#   vfs_mount(manager, "/", fs)                the SHARED Filesystem trait
#                                              surface, via
#                                              dbfs_filesystem_ops.spl's
#                                              `impl Filesystem for DbFsDriver`.
#
# That last step is the point: the round-trip is served through the SAME
# generic Filesystem trait / VfsManager.mount() surface the FAT32 lane uses,
# so `manager.write_text` / `manager.read_text` below are requests served by
# the DBFS filesystem service against a real NVMe device. Nothing here touches
# a host file, and nothing here is a probe-private copy of the driver — it is
# the same adapter boot_fs.spl mounts as root.
#
# WHY THE dbd DATABASE DAEMON IS NOT IMPORTED — load-bearing, not tidiness.
# The obvious move is to route the round-trip through src/os/apps/dbd. Measured
# on this base branch, that does not build: `dbd.spl` and `dbd_provisioning.spl`
# FAIL CODEGEN under strict mode (SIMPLE_ALLOW_STUB_FALLBACK unset), bodies
# [DbdLiveClientSessionV1.create] and [DbdProvisioningOwnerV1.ready], and
# native-build aborts. Setting SIMPLE_ALLOW_STUB_FALLBACK to get past it is NOT
# an option: it emits empty stubs, and a filesystem round-trip "proved" by a
# silently-stubbed binary is exactly the fabricated evidence this lane exists
# to rule out. Pulling dbd in also drags the ring-3 syscall shim, whose
# freestanding link is separately broken by a linker.ld name-mangling mismatch
# (see vfs_server_roundtrip_entry.spl for the nm evidence). The round-trip is
# kernel-resident and needs no ring-3 syscall, so the correct response is to
# not pull it in. dbd is the DB SERVER; dbfs is the FILESYSTEM backed by the
# embedded DB, and it is dbfs this lane is about.
#
# WHAT MAKES THE ROUND-TRIP HONEST — three independent anchors:
#   * TOKEN, read BEFORE any write. The host stages /DBFSRT.TOK carrying THIS
#     run's token (src/os/port/mkfs_dbfsrt.spl). The guest must read it back
#     through the mounted filesystem before it writes anything. This is the
#     rung the FAT32 lane does not have, and it closes a hole nonce-absence
#     cannot: a guest that silently RE-FORMATTED the volume could still write a
#     fresh file and read it back perfectly. On a wiped volume the token is
#     gone, so that scenario fails here, at L5, before the write is attempted.
#   * NONCE, generated per run into build/os/generated/dbfs_roundtrip_nonce.spl
#     at build time. A stale kernel or a stale disk image carries a stale nonce
#     and CANNOT satisfy the gate, which greps for the exact current nonce. The
#     freshly built volume is asserted, before boot, to contain neither the
#     nonce nor any /DBFSRT.TXT entry.
#   * HOST-SIDE image check. After the guest halts the gate searches the raw
#     NVMe image ON THE HOST for the nonce bytes — an independent filesystem
#     check outside the guest that the data really reached the disk.
#
# FLUSH BEFORE HALT: DBFS buffers through its commit owner, so the entry calls
# unmount() (which performs group_commit — see dbfs_filesystem_ops.spl) after
# the read-back. Without that the bytes can be correct in-guest and absent from
# the image, which would make the host-side check a false RED.

use build.os.generated.dbfs_roundtrip_nonce.{dbfs_roundtrip_nonce}
use os.services.vfs.nvme_boot_runtime_owner.{vfs_boot_probe_pure_simple_nvme_adapter}
use os.services.vfs.vfs.{Filesystem, VfsManager, vfs_mount}
use os.services.dbfs.dbfs_filesystem_ops.{}
# WHY `os.kernel.net.thread_shim` IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* IMPORTED HERE.
# thread_shim.spl is SimpleOS's would-be critical-section provider:
# `@export("C") spl_mutex_create/lock/unlock` over a bounded boot arena word
# and an architecture acquire/release CAS, with the lock word carrying
# cpu_id+1. Its SOURCE is right. Its CODEGEN is not. Measured 2026-09-01 by
# importing it here and disassembling the resulting kernel: the seed's
# cranelift freestanding backend does NOT honour `@naked`. Each `asm volatile`
# body is emitted as a separate `__simple_asm_H<hash>` thunk WITH a frame
# prologue prepended --
#     __simple_asm_H9630faa93e1d40d9:
#         push %rbp ; mov %rsp,%rbp ; mov %esi,%eax
#         lock cmpxchgl %edx,(%rdi) ; sete %al ; movzbl %al,%eax ; ret
# -- so the body's own `ret` pops the SAVED RBP as a return address and jumps
# wild; and the caller is lowered as `call *%rsi` followed by `ud2`, so even a
# correctly-returning thunk lands on a trap. Importing thread_shim therefore
# does not give this lane a working lock, it gives it a wild jump during
# module init, BEFORE any serial rung is printed -- strictly worse than the
# clean fail-closed refusal below. Left unimported on purpose until the naked
# lowering is fixed (tail-call, no prologue on either side, return value
# propagated). See
# doc/08_tracking/bug/naked_asm_lowering_miscompiles_simpleos_mutex_2026-09-01.md
use std.db.dbfs_driver.device_commit_owner.{dbfs_device_serialization_blocker}
use std.db.dbfs_engine.superblock.{
    dbfs_superblock_set_device, dbfs_superblock_probe_disk, dbfs_superblock_read_from_disk
}
use std.db.dbfs_driver.dbfs_driver.{DbFsDriver}
use std.fs_driver.error.{FsError}

extern fn serial_println(msg: text)

val DBFSRT_PATH: text  = "/DBFSRT.TXT"
val DBFSRT_TOKEN_PATH: text = "/DBFSRT.TOK"

# Arena base LBA. Must match boot_fs.spl's DBFS_ARENA_BASE_LBA and the layout
# build_dbfs_rootfs_image writes (dbfs_image.spl): LBA 0-1 reserved, 2-3
# superblock replicas, 4+ arena.
val DBFSRT_ARENA_BASE_LBA: i64 = 4

fn _dbfsrt_fs_err_text(err: FsError) -> text:
    """Render an FsError so an in-guest refusal names its own cause."""
    match err:
        case FsError.Unsupported: "unsupported"
        case FsError.Transient(_): "transient"
        case FsError.Corrupt: "corrupt"
        case FsError.NoSpace: "no-space"
        case FsError.Permission: "permission"
        case FsError.NotFound: "not-found"
        case FsError.AlreadyExists: "already-exists"
        case FsError.Busy: "busy"
        case FsError.IoError(code): "io-error:" + code.to_u64().to_text()
        case FsError.Interrupted: "interrupted"
        case FsError.TooLarge: "too-large"
        case FsError.ReadOnly: "read-only"
        case FsError.InvalidArg: "invalid-arg"
        case FsError.StaleHandle: "stale-handle"
        case FsError.PathTraversal: "path-traversal"

fn _start():
    serial_println("")
    serial_println("=== SimpleOS DBFS filesystem-server round-trip probe ===")
    val nonce = dbfs_roundtrip_nonce()
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] probe begin nonce={nonce}")

    # --- 1. NVMe block device (production pure-Simple adapter) --------------
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] acquiring pure-Simple NVMe adapter")
    val dev_result = vfs_boot_probe_pure_simple_nvme_adapter()
    if dev_result.is_err():
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] nvme adapter ok=false err={dev_result.unwrap_err()}")
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] PROBE FAILED: no NVMe block device")
        return
    val dev = dev_result.unwrap()
    # Render bools EXPLICITLY. `"{flag}"` renders a Simple bool as `1`/`0`, not
    # `true`/`false`, which silently missed the sibling lane's needle on a fully
    # successful run (measured 2026-08-31). Do NOT "fix" that by relaxing the
    # gate's needles — its selftest fixtures are written in true/false.
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] nvme adapter ok=true")

    # --- 2. DBFS superblock + driver open (never format) --------------------
    # Deliberately NO dbfs_superblock_format_disk call on any path. This entry
    # only ever OPENS the volume the host staged; a probe that formats on
    # failure would be able to round-trip on a wiped disk.
    dbfs_superblock_set_device(dev)
    if not dbfs_superblock_probe_disk():
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] dbfs superblock found=false")
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] PROBE FAILED: no DBFS superblock at LBA 2")
        return
    val sb = dbfs_superblock_read_from_disk()
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] dbfs superblock found=true gen={sb.mount_generation}")
    # block_count is load-bearing and was the blocker once already: a volume
    # formatted with the plain dbfs_superblock_format_disk carries 0 here, and
    # open_on_device rejects anything under 4. Always report it.
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] dbfs superblock block_count={sb.block_count}")

    # --- 2b. Critical-section provider admission ---------------------------
    # Printed BEFORE the open so a refusal is attributable. "ready" here means
    # the linked provider passed the behavioural handshake in
    # device_commit_owner.spl (create, lock, unlock, DOUBLE UNLOCK MUST FAIL,
    # relock, release); a stubbed provider prints the probe-failed blocker.
    val provider = dbfs_device_serialization_blocker()
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] critical-section provider=" + provider)

    val driver_r = DbFsDriver.open_on_device(dev, DBFSRT_ARENA_BASE_LBA, sb.block_count.to_i64())
    if driver_r.is_err():
        # Two renderings on purpose: the match-based one came back EMPTY in
        # freestanding (measured 2026-09-01), which is itself the documented
        # x86_64-freestanding unwrap/match defect class, so the raw Result
        # interpolation is printed alongside it as a second opinion.
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] dbfs driver open ok=false err=" + _dbfsrt_fs_err_text(driver_r.unwrap_err()))
        # ROOT CAUSE, established 2026-09-01 by i64 stage/diag markers
        # temporarily compiled into the stdlib (see the bug record): the refusal
        # is FsError.Unsupported from dbfs_owner_transaction_begin(), because
        # dbfs_device_mutex_provider_blocker() fail-closes on any platform that
        # is not linux/macos/windows/freebsd/illumos/solaris. SimpleOS is not on
        # that list, deliberately -- DBFS's device commit owner requires an
        # audited runtime-backed mutex provider, and freestanding has none. That
        # check is a real serialization guarantee for a filesystem commit owner
        # and is NOT to be relaxed to turn this lane green.
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] PROBE FAILED: DbFsDriver.open_on_device refused the volume "
            + "(dbfs commit owner has no audited mutex provider on this platform)")
        return
    val driver = driver_r.unwrap()

    # --- 3. Mount through the SHARED Filesystem trait surface ---------------
    var manager = VfsManager.new()
    val fs: Filesystem = driver
    val mount_r = vfs_mount(manager, "/", fs)
    if mount_r.is_err():
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] dbfs mount ok=false err={mount_r.unwrap_err()}")
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] PROBE FAILED: DBFS would not mount through the Filesystem trait")
        return
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] dbfs-server up ready=true")

    # --- 4. TOKEN read-back, BEFORE any write ------------------------------
    # Proves this is the volume the host staged for THIS run, not a re-format.
    val token_r = manager.read_text(DBFSRT_TOKEN_PATH)
    if token_r.is_err():
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] volume token read FAILED: {token_r.unwrap_err()}")
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] PROBE FAILED: staged volume token unreadable (wiped or wrong volume?)")
        return
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] volume token={token_r.unwrap()}")

    # --- 5. The round-trip: DATA through the server -------------------------
    val payload = "dbfsrt-nonce:{nonce}"
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] server write begin path={DBFSRT_PATH} bytes={payload.len()}")
    val wrote = manager.write_text(DBFSRT_PATH, payload)
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] server write path=" + DBFSRT_PATH + " ok=" + (if wrote.is_ok(): "true" else: "false"))
    if wrote.is_err():
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] PROBE FAILED: server refused the write: {wrote.unwrap_err()}")
        return

    val got_r = manager.read_text(DBFSRT_PATH)
    if got_r.is_err():
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] PROBE FAILED: server refused the read-back: {got_r.unwrap_err()}")
        return
    val got = got_r.unwrap()
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] server read-back={got}")
    if got != payload:
        serial_println("[dbfsrt] PROBE FAILED: read-back differs from write")
        return
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] ROUNDTRIP_OK nonce={nonce}")

    # --- 6. Flush to the device so the host-side image check is meaningful --
    val committed = fs.unmount()
    serial_println("[dbfsrt] group-commit ok=" + (if committed.is_ok(): "true" else: "false"))

    serial_println("[dbfsrt] probe complete")

    while true:
        pass

export _start
